      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 1272867615,
      "text": "Schkuhria pinnata, the canchalagua or dwarf Mexican marigold, is a small, dainty, pioneer annual herb of the family Asteraceae and widespread in the tropics. It is regarded as a naturalised weed with low ecological impact."
    },
    "es": {
      "source": "Wikipedia (es): Schkuhria pinnata",
      "source_url": "https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schkuhria_pinnata",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
      "revision": 155232824,
      "text": "Schkuhria pinnata es una planta de la familia Asteraceae. Es una planta utilizada en la medicina tradicional en Sudamérica y África."
    }
  },
  "sections": {
    "uses": {
      "en": {
        "source": "Wikipedia (en): Schkuhria pinnata § Medicinal uses",
        "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schkuhria_pinnata#Medicinal_uses",
        "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
        "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
        "retrieved": "2026-10-17",
        "revision": 1272867615,
        "heading": "Medicinal uses",
        "text": "The Ketchwa indigenous people of the Andes have traditionally used this plant to treat a large number of conditions and ailments - they have used it as a bactericide in open wounds, to treat acne, malaria and inflammation, and as a blood purifier and diuretic. The plant can also be used as an abortifacients by inserting the root or infusing the whole plant and taking it orally.\n\nSchkuhria pinnata exhibited high antibacterial activity while in an anti-inflammatory assay dichloromethane extracts from its shoots selectively inhibited COX-1.",
        "truncated": false
      }
    }
  },
